Booting this kernel on a real Raspberry Pi 3 Model B
=====================================================

On a Pi 3 the ARM cores do not fetch the kernel directly.  The VideoCore
GPU boots first, reads the firmware and config from the first (FAT) SD
partition, loads the ARM kernel image into RAM at 0x80000, and releases
core 0 into it.  So there is no ELF loader on the board: the kernel must
be a raw flat binary, and its startup must cope with the EL the firmware
drops it at.

Build
-----
    export PATH=$ROOT/MacOSX/arm64/bin:$PATH
    cd os/inferno/raspi3b
    mk INIT=raspi3binit install     # builds the ELF kernel (iraspi3b)
    mk kernel8.img                  # -> raw flat image for the firmware

kernel8.img is iraspi3b (ELF) reduced to its loadable segments laid out
at their link addresses, produced by mkrawimg.py from the ELF program
headers.  (7l -H6 emits a headerless image but packs the segments
contiguously, which puts .data at the wrong address; mkrawimg.py honours
the vaddr gap so a flat load at 0x80000 lands .text at 0x80000 and .data
at 0x100000, matching the link.)

FAT boot partition
------------------
Format the first SD partition FAT32 and copy onto it:

    bootcode.bin            }
    start.elf               }  Pi firmware (raspberrypi/firmware, boot/)
    fixup.dat               }
    bcm2710-rpi-3-b.dtb     }
    overlays/disable-bt.dtbo}
    config.txt          (this directory)
    kernel8.img         (mk kernel8.img)

The base .dtb and the overlays directory are not optional even though
this kernel reads no device tree on this board.  dtoverlay= is applied
by start.elf to the board's own .dtb, and a firmware that finds no .dtb
matching the board revision boots in the old ATAG mode and applies no
overlays at all -- silently.  Leave those two files out and everything
still boots, with the PL011 still wired to Bluetooth and the serial
console dead, which looks exactly like a kernel that hung before its
first print.

SD card layout -- READ THIS BEFORE THE FIRST BOOT
------------------------------------------------
raspi3binit writes to the raw card, and where it writes was chosen for a
blank QEMU image with no partition table (tools/boardsuite makes exactly
that: dd of zeroes, no MBR).  On a real card partitioned the ordinary
way, those writes land inside the boot partition and destroy it.

Every raw-card write the init makes, by sector:

    3               sdcheck single-block write/readback  (SDHOST, sdM0)
    5               sdhcicheck single-block              (Arasan, sdH0)
    8-11, 12-15     the two multi-block write/readbacks
    256-8447        sdfs: "part fs 256 8448" and kfs -r reams all of it

Sector 0 is read and never written, so the MBR survives.  The 4MB kfs
partition does not: a Pi card conventionally starts its FAT partition at
sector 2048, and 2048-8447 is inside it.  The board would boot once,
scribble over the FAT's boot sector, its FATs and its root directory,
and never boot again until the card was rewritten.

So partition the card with the FAT boot partition starting at sector
16384 (8MB) or later, leaving sectors 0-8447 unallocated:

    Linux:
        sudo sfdisk /dev/sdX <<'EOT'
        label: dos
        start=16384, size=524288, type=c, bootable
        EOT
        sudo mkfs.vfat -F 32 -n BOOT /dev/sdX1

    macOS: diskutil picks the start offset itself, so use fdisk(8) to
    set it, or write the card on a Linux box.  Do not assume
    "diskutil partitionDisk ... MBR FAT32" put it past 8447; check with
    "sudo fdisk /dev/rdiskX" and look at the partition's start sector.

The firmware does not care where the FAT partition begins -- it reads
the MBR and takes the first FAT partition it finds -- so 16384 costs
nothing.  8MB of the card is simply left for the init to play in.

Nothing here needs a second partition: the kernel carries its own root
(devroot, built from the root list in the raspi3b configuration), so the
FAT partition holds the firmware and kernel8.img and nothing else.

If you would rather not have the card written at all on a bring-up
boot, the three callers are sdhcicheck(), sdcheck() and sdfs() at the
top of os/inferno/init/raspi3binit.b's init(); dropping sdfs() alone
removes every write above sector 15.

config.txt sets arm_64bit=1, kernel=kernel8.img, and routes the PL011
UART (which this kernel's console drives, at 0x3f201000) to the GPIO
header: on a Pi 3 the PL011 is wired to Bluetooth by default, so
dtoverlay=disable-bt hands it back to GPIO 14/15 (header pins 8/10),
115200 8N1.  Attach a 3.3 V USB-serial adapter there for the console.

Exception level
---------------
The firmware enters a 64-bit kernel at EL2 (not EL3).  l.s's core-0 path
reads CurrentEL and drops to EL1 from whichever of EL3 (what QEMU -M
raspi3b gives) or EL2 (real hardware) it finds; an EL3-only drop faults on
the SCR_EL3 write at EL2.

Known hardware gaps (verified only under emulation so far)
----------------------------------------------------------
These are no-ops on QEMU (which models no caches and drains the SD FIFO
synchronously) but matter on the board; grep the sources for the notes:
  - MMU enable does not invalidate the caches first (l.s mmuenable): the
    A53's cache reset state is UNKNOWN and must be invalidated before
    SCTLR.C/I are set.
  - The SDHOST bio now gates each FIFO word on the SDEDM fill level
    (sdhostbcm.c fifospace()/fifodata()), which is real; keep it.
  - Uniprocessor: cores 1-3 are parked at WFE (l.s), MAXMACH is 1.  SMP
    (and the BCM2836 mailbox IPIs) is the next hardware milestone.

Emulator boot that mirrors the firmware
---------------------------------------
QEMU's -kernel wants a Linux Image header for a raw binary, so to exercise
the exact flat image the firmware would load, use an explicit loader that
places it at 0x80000 and starts core 0 there:

    qemu-system-aarch64 -M raspi3b -m 1G -nographic -sd disk.img \
      -device loader,file=kernel8.img,addr=0x80000,force-raw=on \
      -device loader,addr=0x80000,cpu-num=0

(-M raspi3b still enters at EL3 there; the EL2 path above is only taken on
real hardware.)  For day-to-day testing, -kernel iraspi3b (the ELF) is
simpler and equivalent.
